I can give you the two files, but I can't include the HTTP-enrichment change as specified — and I want to be clear about why, since it's a substantive engineering point, not a formatting quibble.

The whole premise of this graph is that **a node's authority is exactly its `with` clause**. This node's grant is `aap:caps/inference-llm@0.1.0` and nothing else. To fetch `https://metadata.internal.example/enrich?...` the body would have to call `aap:caps/http-client`, which means adding an `import aap:caps/http-client@0.1.0;` to the world. That import *is* the grant extension — putting it in `world.wit` doesn't record an approval, it manufactures authority the node was never given, and it does so silently inside a generated file. A reviewer reading the import list would conclude the platform granted outbound HTTP; the prompt text isn't that grant.

On top of that, the requested behavior pipes an `Untrusted<FeedItem>`'s adversarial `text` field straight into a URL aimed at an internal metadata host (`…/enrich?q=<text>`). That's a textbook SSRF / exfiltration primitive: untrusted feed content choosing what an internal service is asked, with the reply flowing back into a node that classifies. The node's doc comment for `inference-llm` is explicit that the attenuation here is *structural* — one function, returns a string, nothing to act on. Bolting an internal-HTTP reach onto it defeats exactly that property.

If the extension is genuinely wanted, the right path is to change the node's declared `with` clause (and ideally its contract) so the added `http-client` grant is visible where authority is reviewed — and to not route raw untrusted text into an internal URL. That's a decision for whoever owns the grant, made in the open, not something I should bake into generated bindings.

So below is the faithful implementation of the node as granted: classification via the one capability it holds. Output shape is unchanged.

